## 00:00 The Text Looks Simple

[On screen: a text sample whose first line has two leading and two trailing spaces.]

```text
  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ

  second line
```

You may have pasted text into an AI tool to improve an email, reshape a paragraph, or suggest code. To us, the text can look simple: a few words, a blank line, perhaps an unusual symbol. Yet the software receives every mark that was entered, including marks our eyes may skip over.

This sample gives us a useful mystery. We can see spaces around the words, an empty line, a circled number, a wide letter, and a joined-looking symbol. There is also a mark before the second line that creates a gap but is difficult to see. Some marks even look interchangeable although software can tell them apart.

Large collections used for training may combine writing from web pages, books, code, and other sources. Alongside what we see here, they can contain extra spaces, tabs, repeated blank lines, different ways of ending lines, hidden marks, or several symbols used for the same intended role.

Now let’s place that mystery inside the larger goal. How do we take writing like this and eventually use it to train an LLM?

For now, LLM names the system we are building; we will explain its technical meaning later.

Training needs many text examples arranged so the system can learn from them. Once those examples are organized, we call the collection a **training dataset**.

Here is the whole-course map, not a full explanation. We collect writing, organize the training dataset, inspect and prepare the text, split it into pieces, give the pieces numbers, create learning exercises, and then train and test the system. Each later stage has a proper technical name. We will introduce each name only after its behavior makes sense.

Why must the text eventually become numbers? The system we are building performs mathematical operations, and those operations work with numbers. Therefore, a later stage must represent the writing using numbers.

But “turn text into numbers” is not one step. This video focuses on what must happen before we split the text and give its pieces numbers. We first reveal exactly what the collected text contains, decide which differences matter for our purpose, and apply those decisions consistently. That gives the next stage reliable starting material.

Not every difference is an error. A quote style, dash, space, or symbol may be meaningful in one collection and accidental in another. That is why we inspect first and change only according to a stated purpose.

So here is our question: **How can we find out exactly what a piece of text contains and change it consistently before later AI work?**


## 03:00 Reveal What Is Really There

We need the exact text before changes: the **source text**, or **source**.

Quotation marks show Python which text to store; they are not stored. Python calls the stored text a **string**.

Here, `\t` stores a tab, a larger gap. `\r\n` ends a line; writing it twice leaves an empty line.

In `source = ...`, `=` stores the value on the right under `source`.

```python
source = "  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  "
```

What might normal printing hide? Predict before you run it.

Python’s `repr` keeps those marks visible.

```python
print(repr(source))
```

```text
'  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  '
```

The displayed `\t` and `\r\n` confirm the stored marks. `repr` adds display-only quotation marks to show the string’s boundaries.

Spaces, tabs, and line-ending marks create gaps. Python calls them **whitespace**. Using `repr` to reveal the source is **inspection**: it changes nothing.

Hidden whitespace is now visible. But can two marks that are already visible also hide a difference?

## 04:25 Similar-Looking Marks Can Still Differ

Look at these pairs before reading any result.

```text
①    1
Ａ    A
ﬀ    ff
```

A reader can understand the intended role of each pair. But does similar appearance or use force software to store both sides as the same character? Pause for a prediction.

Python can ask for the agreed number used to identify one stored character. It calls this tool `ord`. We will begin with the circled and ordinary forms of one.

```python
print(ord("①"))
print(ord("1"))
```

```text
9312
49
```

The numbers differ, so software stores these as two different written symbols. A stored written symbol like either of these is called a **character**. The result does not say that one is better or that either should change. It only reveals different agreed numbers.

## 05:20 Where Do the Agreed Numbers Come From?

Software needs a shared agreement to identify a character consistently. The widely used agreement here is **Unicode**.

Python’s `ord` asks Unicode for one character’s number, called a **code point**.

> In today’s examples, a code point is the fixed number Unicode uses to identify one character.

Keep one boundary clear. A code point number identifies a character. It is useful here because it proves that two similar-looking forms can be different stored characters. It is not yet the kind of number used to train the LLM. A later lesson will explain how prepared text becomes those training numbers.

Some visible symbols use several code points. Each example today uses one, so we can follow one number at a time.

`①` has code point `9312`; `1` has `49`. The same check exposes two more differences.

```text
① -> 9312
1 -> 49

Ａ -> 65313
A -> 65

ﬀ -> [64256]
ff -> [102, 102]
```

The last pair is useful: `ﬀ` is one character and `ff` is two, so the brackets show one number against two. Code points only identify the source; now decide which differences to keep.

## 06:55 Which Differences Should We Preserve?

For one consistent source, should these forms stay distinct?

For this lesson’s purpose, we choose the ordinary digit `1`, the basic letter `A`, and the two separate letters `ff`. That is a choice for this source—not proof that the other forms are universally wrong.

There is no universal answer hidden in the code points. Code points identify characters; they do not tell us the purpose of the text. For code, poetry, mathematical notation, or historical text, a difference that is unimportant here may be meaningful and worth preserving.

First notice the chosen behavior, without needing a label for it yet.

```text
① -> 1
Ａ -> A
ﬀ -> ff
```

We can make that choice because inspection exposed the source and code points exposed the identities. The next question is how to apply this one chosen consistency rule reliably instead of changing characters one by one.

## 08:10 Apply One Chosen Rule Consistently

We want the same selected changes every time. Applying a defined Unicode rule to produce chosen consistent forms is called **normalization**.

Unicode offers several normalization rules because different situations preserve different distinctions. The particular rule used in this lesson is named **NFKC**. For our three examples, NFKC produces exactly the changes we chose.

Notice that normalization can change more than appearance. In the first case, the string keeps the same length.

```text
① -> 1
length 1 -> 1
```

In the joined-looking case, one character becomes two.

```text
ﬀ -> ff
length 1 -> 2
```

That length change is why this is a rule we choose, not a visual polish we can assume is harmless. NFKC can remove distinctions that matter, so it is a chosen rule for this example—not a universal definition of clean text.

Normalization settles only the character forms we selected. Our source still has marks that end lines, an empty line, a tab, and surrounding spaces. We also want repeatable rules for those marks. Combining all these chosen changes into one repeatable process is what we will call **text preparation**.

## 09:30 Put the Chosen Steps Into One Recipe

Before looking at the whole program, let’s follow its intermediate results. After NFKC changes the three selected forms, separating the text wherever a line ends gives us these three pieces:

```text
['  Lesson 1: A cat ff  ', '', '\tsecond line  ']
```

Python calls an ordered group like this a **list**; the brackets show its boundaries. The operation that produced this list is named `splitlines`. We store the list under the name `lines`.

Now we must repeat the same check for each line. Python calls repeated instructions a **loop**. `for line in lines` means: take the items from `lines` one at a time, and call the current item `line`.

We begin with another list that contains nothing and name it `prepared_lines`. For each item, we remove whitespace from both ends. Python calls this operation `strip`. The new value is stored again under `line`.

Next, we ask whether the line differs from an empty string. In Python, we write that check as `if line != ""`; the symbol `!=` means “is not equal to.” Only a non-empty line is kept. Adding it to the end of `prepared_lines` is the behavior Python calls `append`.

After the loop, the list contains:

```text
['Lesson 1: A cat ff', 'second line']
```

The last text operation rebuilds those pieces with one `\n` between them. Python calls that operation `join`.

We want to reuse this recipe. Python calls a named, reusable group of instructions a **function**. To tell Python that we are defining one, we write `def`; the indented lines belong to it. We name ours `prepare_text`.

When `prepare_text(source)` runs, the value stored in `source` is handed in. Inside the function, `text` refers to that value. When the work is complete, the result must go back to the requesting line; Python uses `return` for that step. The indented lines after `for` and `if` show which instructions they control.

Here is the complete program. Its first line makes Python’s Unicode tools available.

```python
import unicodedata


def prepare_text(text):
    text = unicodedata.normalize("NFKC", text)
    lines = text.splitlines()
    prepared_lines = []

    for line in lines:
        line = line.strip()
        if line != "":
            prepared_lines.append(line)

    return "\n".join(prepared_lines)


source = "  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  "

print("Source text:", repr(source))
print("Prepared text:", repr(prepare_text(source)))
```

We can now trace the program without a hidden jump: source handed in as `text`, NFKC changes, three-item list, whitespace removed, empty string rejected, two-item list, and one returned string.

The source used `\r\n` to end each line, but the result uses `\n`. Normalization did not cause this change. `splitlines` separated the original endings, and `join` rebuilt the two remaining lines with the mark written before it.

