## 14:05 Carry the Explanation Forward

We can now answer the central question. The source is the unchanged starting text. Inspection reveals its marks. A code point identifies one character. Normalization applies a chosen Unicode rule. Text preparation combines that rule with changes to lines and whitespace.

Identification tells us what is present; preparation applies our decisions. We inspected first, chose NFKC with its limit in view, and traced every step.

This order prevents a common mistake: assuming every difference should disappear. A difference can be real, while the purpose determines whether we preserve or change it.

With new text, start the same way. Reveal its marks, identify relevant differences, choose rules that fit the purpose, and test changed starting text. Prepared text is now a dependable building block for the next lesson.
